    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.02-09",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A contour interval is vertical, while contour spacing on the map is horizontal. The former helps determine height changes; the latter, with scale, helps judge slope. Close spacing can indicate a steep slope where intervals are equal. Counting contour lines without checking their labelled interval can produce a wrong height difference. Spot heights give elevations at particular points, not the general height of every nearby symbol.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.02-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.02-01-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.02-01-2",
        "CL-6137-4.2.02-02-1",
