    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.2.02-11",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A route example shows why the requested distance matters. Suppose the direct A–B separation is four centimetres on a valid 1:50,000 print, while the mapped road between them follows six centimetres. Straight ground distance is two kilometres and road distance three. Both are correct for different questions. The longer route is not a mistake in the scale; it follows a different path.\n\n![Measuring straight distance, route, area and gradient at 1:50,000](../../assets/images/ITEM-6137-4.2-008-INTERP-answer.png)",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.2.02-1",
        "CL-6137-4.2.02-2",
